# Test: `directive scheduler single` — the SPACE-SEPARATED value spelling — is
# refused. A directive value is written `directive name=value`; the grammar has
# no space-separated form (`docs/language-reference.md`, "Directives").
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE CELL THAT WAS NOT MERELY OVER-ACCEPTED BUT ACTED ON. The
# self-host parser carried a branch that consumed a bare token after the name as
# the value, so `directive scheduler single` SELECTED the N:1 single-threaded
# scheduler — measured: the emitted C carried `── Scheduler: Single ──` — while
# Rust gg refused the very same source at parse time. A program whose observable
# interleaving differs between the two compilers, from a spelling only one of
# them admits.
#
# It is a DIFFERENT parser arm from `directive_value_int_error.gg` (that one is
# the `=`-branch value check; this one is the trailing-token check), so one does
# not cover the other. After the fix the `single` token is no longer consumed and
# the directive reduces to a valueless `scheduler`, which the semantic pass then
# also refuses — but the trailing-token check is what fires first, and it is what
# makes `directive trace single` (see `directive_trailing_token_error.gg`) a
# reject too, where the valueless form alone would have been accepted.
#
# Pinned on BOTH lanes: `directive_space_separated_value_error` (Rust gg) and
# `sh_rejects_directive_space_separated_value` (self-host driver).

directive scheduler single

void main():
    print("REACHED — the space-separated directive value was NOT refused")
